{
  "_source": "Fisher 657 Diaphragm Actuator instruction manual D100306X012, June 2018, page 24, Figure 6 -- which the manual states \"shows the size 30 through 60\". Traced by the 657 Working Model pipeline (20 - Source Library/Interactive Models/Fisher 657 Working Model/pipeline): Franz hand-painted the part masks over a 600-DPI crop, masks-from-paint.py assigned them, refine2.py contoured and denoised them. These are that pipeline's OUTPUT, copied, not a second tracing of the same figure.",
  "_frame": "Inches. x from the actuator centreline, y UP from the bottom face of the yoke -- which is the face that lands on the valve bonnet, so it is the datum an assembly needs. The source frame is the 1480x2250 crop at 186.667 px/in, a scale the model declares itself (140 px = 0.75 in of rated travel).",
  "_note": "ONE FIGURE, SEVEN SIZES. The manual says Figure 6 covers sizes 30 through 60, so this outline serves all of them; what differs between the sizes is in the catalogue rows -- effective area, yoke boss, stem, travel, thrust -- not in the drawing. The shape is drawn at the size 30 proportions it was traced from, and a consumer wanting another size scales it by the ratio of the two yoke bosses, which is the dimension the mounting is built on. AND THE DRAWING IS NOT DIMENSIONED TO ONE SIZE. At the scale the model declares, the traced casing comes out about 6.9 in across, where a size 30 effective area of 46 in2 implies 7.65 in. That is what a family figure is: it shows the ARRANGEMENT of seven actuators, not the dimensions of any one. So the shape is representative and the catalogue rows are authoritative, and nothing should read a size off this outline.",
  "pxPerInch": 186.66666666666666,
  "centrelinePx": 742.5,
